==============================================================================
A4  sign convention: which alpha strengthens gravity along a channel
==============================================================================
   FIRST the analytic anchor.  For a UNIFORM K = diag(k_par,1,1)
   the exact monopole Psi = -GM/(sqrt(det K) sqrt(r^T K^-1 r))
   gives |g|_along / |g|_across = sqrt(k_par/k_perp) at equal r.
   So LOWERING the conductivity along an axis WEAKENS |g| along it:
   flux is diverted into the better-conducting transverse
   directions faster than the extra resistance raises the
   gradient.  (The opposite intuition -- 'poor conductor needs a
   bigger gradient' -- is only right when there is nowhere to
   divert to, i.e. for a RADIALLY ALIGNED K in spherical symmetry,
   where |g| ~ k_rad^-1 Newtonian and k_rad^-3/4 deep-MOND.  Both
   facts are true and they are not in conflict; the map uses the
   second, this gate pins the first.)

      k_par=0.4   measured along/across 0.6228   analytic sqrt(k_par/k_perp) 0.6325
      k_par=2.5   measured along/across 1.5662   analytic sqrt(k_par/k_perp) 1.5811
      largest departure from the analytic value: 1.52%

   NOW the tube.  Two 1e13 Msun wells 1.2 Mpc apart on the z axis,
   a Gaussian source between them, mu = 1 so only K acts.  Each run
   is normalised by the same source solved at K = I.

      K = exp[-alpha C]  (alpha>0) |g|/|g_K=I|  along 1.0075  across 1.0162   ratio 0.9914
      K = exp[+alpha C]            |g|/|g_K=I|  along 0.9909  across 0.9838   ratio 1.0072
   [PASS] A4 sign convention fixed by measurement, not by intuition
          uniform-K anchor reproduces sqrt(k_par/k_perp) to 1.52%
          K = exp[-alpha C], alpha > 0 (the brief's sign): the response along
            the channel relative to across it is 0.9914 < 1 -- gravity is WEAKER
            along the line joining the wells and stronger transverse to it.
          K = exp[+alpha C]: 1.0072 > 1 -- this is the sign that makes the
            response STRONGER ALONG THE CHANNELS.
          Both are implemented as sign=-1 and sign=+1 and both are scanned in
          the mechanism map.  The naive argument (a poorer conductor needs a
          larger gradient) is wrong here because flux can be diverted; it is
          right only where no diversion is possible, i.e. for a radially aligned
          K in spherical symmetry, which is the regime the shell-averaged
          boost lives in.

